# Module 04 — Functions

## What you will learn

Functions allow a Rust program to separate logic into reusable units.

## Basic Syntax

```rust
fn greet(name: &str) {
    println!("Hello, {}", name);
}
```

Parameters require types. A return type is written using `->`:

```rust
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}
```

The final expression can become the return value when it has no semicolon.

```rust
fn square(value: i32) -> i32 {
    value * value
}
```

With a semicolon it becomes a statement instead of the function's final expression.

## Expressions vs Statements

Rust is expression-oriented. Blocks can produce values:

```rust
let result = {
    let a = 10;
    let b = 20;
    a + b
};
```

## Practice

Create separate functions for arithmetic operations and a function that determines whether a number is even.

fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}

fn is_even(value: i32) -> bool {
    value % 2 == 0
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", add(10, 20));
    println!("{}", is_even(42));
}

