# Module 05 — Collections

## What you will learn

Real applications rarely work only with fixed-size arrays. Rust provides collections such as vectors, strings, and hash maps.

## Vec<T>

A `Vec<T>` is a growable collection:

```rust
let mut numbers = Vec::new();
numbers.push(10);
numbers.push(20);
```

You can also use:

```rust
let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3, 4];
```

## Accessing Values

Indexing is straightforward:

```rust
let numbers = vec![10, 20, 30];
println!("{}", numbers[0]);
```

For safer access, use `get`:

```rust
let value = numbers.get(10);
```

This returns an `Option`, which avoids an invalid index silently producing undefined behavior.

## HashMap

A `HashMap<K, V>` stores key-value pairs:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

let mut users = HashMap::new();
users.insert("ndk", 21);
```

## Iteration

```rust
for number in &numbers {
    println!("{}", number);
}
```

The `&numbers` is important because the collection is borrowed rather than consumed.

## Practice

Build a small in-memory user registry using a `HashMap` and a list of users using `Vec`.

use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() {
    let mut languages = vec!["Go", "Rust", "Python"];
    languages.push("TypeScript");

    for language in &languages {
        println!("{}", language);
    }

    let mut versions = HashMap::new();
    versions.insert("Rust", "2021");
    versions.insert("Go", "1.22");

    println!("{:?}", versions.get("Rust"));
}

