# Module 08 — Structs, Enums & Pattern Matching

## Structs

Structs allow related values to be grouped into a meaningful type.

```rust
struct User {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}
```

Create a value:

```rust
let user = User {
    name: String::from("Ndk"),
    age: 21,
};
```

## Methods

Methods are defined using `impl`:

```rust
impl User {
    fn introduce(&self) {
        println!("I am {}", self.name);
    }
}
```

`&self` means the method borrows the instance.

## Associated Functions

Functions inside an `impl` block do not need `self`:

```rust
impl User {
    fn new(name: String, age: u32) -> Self {
        Self { name, age }
    }
}
```

They are called with `User::new(...)`.

## Enums

Enums represent one of several possible variants:

```rust
enum Status {
    Active,
    Disabled,
}
```

Variants can contain data:

```rust
enum Message {
    Text(String),
    Number(i32),
}
```

## Pattern Matching

`match` can inspect enum variants and extract their values.

This becomes especially important with `Option` and `Result`.

## Practice

Create a `User` struct and an enum representing account state. Write methods and use `match` to display the state.

enum Status {
    Active,
    Disabled,
}

struct User {
    name: String,
    status: Status,
}

impl User {
    fn new(name: String) -> Self {
        Self {
            name,
            status: Status::Active,
        }
    }

    fn print_status(&self) {
        match &self.status {
            Status::Active => println!("Active"),
            Status::Disabled => println!("Disabled"),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let user = User::new(String::from("Ndk"));
    println!("{}", user.name);
    user.print_status();
}

