# Module 09 — Option & Result

## Option<T>

Rust does not use null in the same way as many mainstream languages. Optional values are represented with `Option<T>`.

```rust
enum Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None,
}
```

For example:

```rust
fn find_user(id: u32) -> Option<String> {
    if id == 1 {
        Some(String::from("Ndk"))
    } else {
        None
    }
}
```

Handle it using `match`:

```rust
match find_user(1) {
    Some(name) => println!("{}", name),
    None => println!("User not found"),
}
```

## Result<T, E>

Operations that can succeed or fail commonly return `Result`:

```rust
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(E),
}
```

Example:

```rust
fn divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> Result<i32, String> {
    if b == 0 {
        Err(String::from("cannot divide by zero"))
    } else {
        Ok(a / b)
    }
}
```

## The ? Operator

The `?` operator propagates an error instead of requiring a large `match` at every level.

```rust
fn read_value() -> Result<String, std::io::Error> {
    let value = std::fs::read_to_string("data.txt")?;
    Ok(value)
}
```

If reading fails, the error is returned immediately.

## unwrap

`unwrap()` extracts a successful value but panics when the value is an error.

It is useful in controlled examples, but production code should normally handle failures deliberately.

## Practice

Write functions that return both `Option` and `Result`. Handle `Some`/`None` and `Ok`/`Err` explicitly.

fn divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> Result<i32, String> {
    if b == 0 {
        return Err(String::from("cannot divide by zero"));
    }

    Ok(a / b)
}

fn find_user(id: u32) -> Option<&'static str> {
    if id == 1 {
        Some("Ndk")
    } else {
        None
    }
}

fn main() {
    match divide(10, 2) {
        Ok(value) => println!("Result: {}", value),
        Err(error) => println!("Error: {}", error),
    }

    match find_user(1) {
        Some(name) => println!("User: {}", name),
        None => println!("Not found"),
    }
}

