# Module 11 — Traits & Generics

## Why Generics?

Generics allow code to work with multiple types without duplicating the implementation.

```rust
fn identity<T>(value: T) -> T {
    value
}
```

`T` represents a type chosen by the caller.

## Traits

Traits define behavior that a type can implement.

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn summary(&self) -> String;
}
```

A type implements it using:

```rust
impl Summary for User {
    fn summary(&self) -> String {
        format!("User: {}", self.name)
    }
}
```

## Trait Bounds

A generic function can require that a type implements a trait:

```rust
fn print_summary<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("{}", item.summary());
}
```

This tells the compiler that `T` must provide the `Summary` behavior.

## where

Complex bounds can be made more readable:

```rust
fn print<T>(value: T)
where
    T: Summary,
{
    println!("{}", value.summary());
}
```

## Associated Types

Traits can define associated types when the implementation determines a related type.

## Practice

Create a trait for objects that can be displayed as a summary. Implement it for at least two different structs and write a generic function that accepts anything implementing the trait.

trait Summary {
    fn summary(&self) -> String;
}

struct User {
    name: String,
}

impl Summary for User {
    fn summary(&self) -> String {
        format!("User: {}", self.name)
    }
}

fn print_summary<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("{}", item.summary());
}

fn main() {
    let user = User {
        name: String::from("Ndk"),
    };

    print_summary(&user);
}

